For bounded real $(1,1)$-forms, Hodge heat is well defined by cutoff limits of the complete $L^2$ semigroup. It preserves order and obeys $$-Cg\le A\le Cg\quad\mathrel{%
  \BeginAccSupp{method=hex,unicode,space=false,pdfliteral=direct,ActualText=27F9}%
  \Longrightarrow%
  \EndAccSupp{pdfliteral=direct}%
}\quad
 -Cg\le\mathcal H_aA\le Cg,
 \qquad \mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_g\mathcal H_aA=H_a\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_g A.$$ If $A$ is also smooth and closed, then $$\label{prelim:hodge-identity}
 \mathcal H_aA
   =A+\partial\bar\partial\int_0^aH_b\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_g A\,db.$$ If a Lipschitz function $f$ is constant off a compact set and $\partial\bar\partial f\le Cg$ as currents, then $$\label{prelim:hessian-preservation}
 \partial\bar\partial H_af\le Cg\qquad(a>0).$$

There is a smooth Kähler–Ricci flow $h_t$ on $M$, for $0\le t<\infty$, with $h_0=g$. There are smooth real functions $U,P$ and $\rho$ such that $$\label{flow:identities}
 \begin{gathered}
 0<h_t\le g,\qquad
 h_t=g-t\mathop{\mathrm{Ric}}(g)+\partial\bar\partial U,\qquad U(\cdot,0)=0,\\
 P=U_t=\log\frac{\det h_t}{\det g},\qquad
 \rho=-P\ge0,\qquad R_{h_t}=-P_t\ge0.
 \end{gathered}$$ For each finite $T$, there is $C_T$ such that $$\label{flow:rho-average}
 \sup_{0\le t\le T}
 \frac{1}{\mathop{\mathrm{Vol}}_gB_g(o,L)}\int_{B_g(o,L)}\rho(x,t)\,dV_g(x)
 \le C_TL\qquad(L\ge1).$$ At every fixed $x$, $\rho(x,t)\le C_x(1+t)$ for all $t\ge0$. Furthermore, on each finite time interval there is a smooth positive function $Q$ and a constant $c>0$ with $$\label{flow:proper-supercaloric}
 Q\ge c(1+r^2),\qquad
 (\partial_t-\Delta_{h_t})Q\ge c\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_{h_t}g.$$ Smoothness at $t=0$ means smoothness with all one-sided time derivatives.

For the flow of Theorem <a href="#thm:localized-flow" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:localized-flow">4</a>, $$\label{disc:symplectic-evolution}
 \partial_tN=p(\partial\bar\partial_YN).$$ For every real smooth base function $d=d(x,t)$, with $t$ held fixed in the spatial Hessian, $$\label{disc:base-addition}
 p(\partial\bar\partial_Y(N+d))=\partial_tN+\Delta_{h_t}d.$$ These identities do not assume that $h_t$ is complete or has nonnegative curvature at positive time.

Let $f$ be a holomorphic disc in $Y$ extending past the unit circle. There is a holomorphic frame $E_f$ of $f^*TY$, smooth on the closed disc, for which $U=dG\,E_f$ satisfies $$\label{disc:frame-unitary}
 U^*U=I\quad\hbox{on }\partial\Delta,\qquad
 \sup_\Delta\|U\|_{\mathrm{op}}\le1.$$ Let $\mathfrak g:Y\to\mathop{\mathrm{Gr}}(2n,q)$ send $y$ to $dG(T_yY)$, and normalize the Plücker form by $\omega_{\mathop{\mathrm{Gr}}}=i\partial\bar\partial\log\det(V^*V)$ for a local holomorphic frame $V$ of its tautological plane. Then $$\label{disc:gauss-area}
 D(U)^2=\frac1{2\pi}\int_\Delta
                          (\mathfrak g\circ f)^*\omega_{\mathop{\mathrm{Gr}}}.$$ If $f(\partial\Delta)\subset K$ for a fixed compact $K\subset Y$, the holomorphic component matrix $J_{E_f}$ of $\mathcal J$ in this frame is bounded throughout $\Delta$ by a constant depending only on $K$ and $G$. If also $f(0)$ ranges in a compact subset $K_0$ of a fixed coordinate chart, the frame matrix at zero and its inverse are bounded by constants depending only on $K$, $K_0$, $G$ and that chart. These constants do not depend on the disc area or on an interior compact containing its image.

The maximum in <a href="#ell:optimizer" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ell:optimizer">[ell:optimizer]</a> exists and is unique. The function $\mathcal L$ is lower semicontinuous in each holomorphic coordinate chart, and $$\label{ell:volume-gap}
 F_0:=\log\det b_t-\mathcal L
 \quad\text{satisfies}\quad 0\le F_0\le\rho.$$ At any fixed point, choose linear cotangent coordinates in which $B=I$. There are finitely many unit columns $\xi_i$ and positive numbers $\alpha_i$ such that $$\label{ell:contact-resolution}
 v(x,\xi_i,t)=1,\qquad
 \sum_{i=1}^m\alpha_i\xi_i\xi_i^*=I,
 \qquad \sum_{i=1}^m\alpha_i=n.$$ The largest weight can be made arbitrarily small by repeating columns.

For the optimizer in <a href="#ell:optimizer" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="ell:optimizer">[ell:optimizer]</a>, every smooth lower test $\lambda$ of $\mathcal L$ at an interior point $(x_0,t_0)$ satisfies $$\label{ell:volume-viscosity}
 \partial_t\lambda(x_0,t_0)
 \ge e^{-F_0(x_0,t_0)}\Delta_{h_{t_0}}\lambda(x_0,t_0).$$ Thus $\mathcal L$ satisfies this inequality in the lower viscosity sense on $M\times(0,T)$.

The flow of Theorem <a href="#thm:localized-flow" data-reference-type="ref" data-reference="thm:localized-flow">4</a> has nonnegative holomorphic bisectional curvature and is complete at every positive time. Its Ricci tensor is positive definite. Writing $A=\mathop{\mathrm{Ric}}(h_t)$ and $R=\mathop{\mathrm{tr}}_{h_t}A$, one has, in fixed holomorphic coordinates, $$\begin{aligned}
 A_t+A h_t^{-1}A+t^{-1}A&\ge0,
 \label{chart:matrix-harnack}\\
 R_t+t^{-1}R+2\operatorname{Re}\partial_XR+A(X,\overline X)&\ge0
 \qquad(X\in T^{1,0}M).
 \label{chart:scalar-harnack}
\end{aligned}$$ In particular $h_{t'}\le h_t$ for $t'\ge t$, $R(o,t)\le C$ for $t\ge0$, and $$\label{chart:integrated-harnack}
 R(x,t)\le R(y,t')\frac{t'}t
 \exp\!\left(\frac{C\mathop{\mathrm{dist}}_{h_t}(x,y)^2}{t'-t}\right)
 \qquad(0<t<t').$$ On each fixed compact set, $h_t\le C_Kt^{-a_K}g$ for $t\ge1$, with $a_K>0$.

The function $q$ is nondecreasing, $q\ge c>0$ for large $s$, and $$\label{chart:q-growth}
 s\mathrel{%
  \BeginAccSupp{method=hex,unicode,space=false,pdfliteral=direct,ActualText=27F6}%
  \longrightarrow%
  \EndAccSupp{pdfliteral=direct}%
}\infty,\qquad
 q\le Ce^\tau,\qquad
 \liminf_{s\to\infty}\frac{q(s)}s\le2.$$ Uniformly on every fixed compact set, $\rho(x,t)/s\to1$, and there is $c_K>0$ such that, eventually on that compact set, $$\label{chart:metric-decay}
 e^{-2s}g\le h_t\le e^{-c_Ks}g.$$ There are fixed $a_0\in(0,1)$ and $b_0>0$ such that the least eigenvalue at $o$ of $t\mathop{\mathrm{Ric}}(h_t)$ relative to $h_t$ satisfies $$\label{chart:ricci-lower}
 \lambda_{\min}(s)\ge b_0q(a_0s)
 \quad\text{for all sufficiently large }s.$$ Moreover there are $S_i\to\infty$ and $K_0<\infty$ with $$\label{chart:good-windows}
 q(2S_i)\le K_0q(a_0S_i).$$ One can choose $s_i\in[5S_i/4,7S_i/4]$ so that $(\log q)'(s_i)\to0$. If $t_i$ corresponds to $s_i$ and $R_i=R(o,t_i)$, the rescaled flows $$\label{chart:rescaled-flows}
 g_i(z)=R_i h_{t_i+z/R_i}$$ have uniform curvature and derivative bounds on each fixed-radius moving ball about $o$ in a fixed small two-sided time interval. At $(o,0)$ their scalar curvature is one and their Ricci eigenvalues have a fixed positive lower bound.

For every sufficiently large volume clock $s$, there is a holomorphic local biholomorphism $\Phi_s:B_{\mathbb C^n}(r_0)\to M$ centered at $o$, with unitary center derivative for the metric at clock $s$, and $$\label{chart:uniform-chart-metric}
 C^{-1}g_{\rm eucl}\le\Phi_s^*h_{t(s)}\le Cg_{\rm eucl}.$$ For fixed $0<r_1<r_0$, chosen sufficiently small, the transition germs $F_{s,u}=\Phi_u^{-1}\Phi_s$, $u\ge s$, extend to nonsingular holomorphic maps on $B(r_1)$ with a common bound. They fix $0$, compose as germs, and satisfy $$\label{chart:center-transitions}
 \|F_{s,u}'(0)\|\le1,\qquad
 |\det F_{s,u}'(0)|=e^{-(u-s)/2},\qquad
 \|F_{s,u}'(0)^{-1}\|\le e^{(u-s)/2}.$$ There are coherent inverse branches $f_s$ on neighborhoods of any fixed compact exhaustion set for all sufficiently large $s$, satisfying $f_s(o)=0$ and $\Phi_sf_s=\mathop{\mathrm{Id}}$. A constant $\sigma>0$, common to all compact sets, satisfies $$\label{chart:common-exponent}
 \sup_K|f_s|\le e^{-\sigma s}
 \quad\text{for every fixed compact }K
 \text{ and all sufficiently large }s.$$ For every fixed jet order $d$ there is $C_d$ such that $$\label{chart:transition-jets}
 |J_dF_{s,u}|\le C_d\min\{1,e^{C_ds-\sigma u}\}
 \qquad(u\ge s\text{ sufficiently large}).$$ There are $c,\gamma_0>0$ such that, for each fixed $0<\gamma\le\gamma_0$, one has $$\label{chart:transition-small-balls}
 \sup_{|z|\le e^{-\gamma s}}|F_{s,u}(z)|
 \le e^{-c\gamma u}
 \qquad\text{for every }u\ge s\ge s_\gamma.$$ The constants in the last two estimates are independent of the later endpoint $u$; the starting threshold in <a href="#chart:common-exponent" data-reference-type="eqref" data-reference="chart:common-exponent">[chart:common-exponent]</a> may depend on $K$.

Fix an integer $d\ge2$ and a macro width $0<h<1$. After starting at a sufficiently late macro endpoint, there is a grid $s_k\to\infty$ with macro endpoints $S_j=e^{hj}$ and the following properties. Each macro interval is either one bad step or is divided into regular steps of length comparable to $\sqrt{S_j}$. Thus, eventually, $$c_h\sqrt{s_k}\le\Delta s_k:=s_{k+1}-s_k
 \le(e^h-1)s_k.
 \label{eq:dyn:grid-size}$$ For $F_k=F_{s_k,s_{k+1}}$ there are polynomial local coordinate changes $H_k$ and polynomial automorphisms $G_k$ of $\mathbb C^n$ satisfying $$J_d(H_{k+1}\circ F_k)=J_d(G_k\circ H_k).
 \label{eq:dyn:jet-conjugacy}$$ The degrees of $H_k,G_k,G_k^{-1}$ are bounded independently of $k$. The coefficients of $H_k$ and its inverse jet are $\exp(o(s_k))$; $H_k'(0)$ and its inverse have polynomial bounds in $s_k$. For a constant $C_d$ depending only on the fixed jet order and dimension, $$\begin{aligned}
 \lVert G_k\rVert_{\mathrm{coeff}}+\lVert G_k^{-1}\rVert_{\mathrm{coeff}}
 &\le \exp\bigl(C_d\Delta s_k+o(s_k)\bigr),
 \label{eq:dyn:coefficient-bound}\\
 \lVert G_k'(0)^{-1}\rVert
 &\le s_k^{C}\exp(\Delta s_k/2).
 \label{eq:dyn:center-inverse}
\end{aligned}$$ In particular, the coefficient $C_d$ of $\Delta s_k$ is independent of $h$. Finally, for some finite $D$, $$\deg G_{0,k}\le D s_k
 \quad\text{on an unbounded subsequence of macro endpoints.}
 \label{eq:dyn:forward-degree}$$
